<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">All-Russian Congress of Soviets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The All-Russian Congress of Soviets was the supreme body of power in Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1917 until the late 1930s. Its Second Congress, meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917, approved the Bolshevik seizure of power, set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> and adopted the decrees on peace and land. The Third Congress in January 1918 proclaimed the Russian Soviet Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-russian-congress-of-soviets.jpg" alt="All-Russian Congress of Soviets" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Russian_Soviet_Federative_Socialist_Republic_(1918%E2%80%931920).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first soviets appeared during the 1905 Russian Revolution as councils of workers in cities gripped by mass strikes, where the enterprises on strike sent delegates to coordinate their actions. By October 1905 the name Soviet of Workers' Deputies had become the most common, and soviets of soldiers, sailors and peasants followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and Socialist-Revolutionaries saw them as strike committees or organs of local self-government, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> called the soviet the germ of a provisional revolutionary government. In his April Theses of 1917 he called for all power to pass to the soviets, which shared a dual power with the Provisional Government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An All-Russian conference of soviets met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> on 11–16 April 1917 with 480 delegates, and the First All-Russian Congress of Soviets of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies sat from 16 June to 7 July 1917. Of its 777 delegates who declared a party, 285 were Socialist-Revolutionaries and 248 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> against 105 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, and it confirmed the supremacy of the Provisional Government. At the Second Congress, after Julius Martov had proposed a coalition of all socialist parties, the right-wing Mensheviks and Socialist-Revolutionaries denounced the insurrection and walked out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> told the advocates of compromise to go into the dustbin of history.</p><p>The Fourth Congress, in March 1918, ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, and the Left Socialist-Revolutionaries, who voted against it, withdrew their ministers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a>. The Left SR Uprising broke out during the Fifth Congress in July 1918, and its suppression ended their part in the congresses. Later sessions met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>: the Eighth, in December 1920, heard Gleb Krzhizhanovsky's report on the GOELRO plan for electrification, and at the Tenth, in December 1922, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> announced the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, Belorussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Delegates were sent by the city soviets, one for every 25,000 voters, and by the provincial and autonomous republican congresses, one for every 125,000 inhabitants. The 1918 Constitution of the Russian SFSR required the Congress to meet at least twice a year. It alone elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a>, adopted and amended the constitution and approved the constitutions of the autonomous republics; on other questions it shared authority with that committee. Several parties competed in its early sessions, but its authority shrank during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> stamp. The constitutional reform of 1936–1937 replaced it with the directly elected Supreme Soviet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “All-Russian Congress of Soviets” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Congress of Soviets</b> approved a new government, the Council of People’s Commissars (Sovnarkom), chaired by Lenin, and adopted decrees on peace and land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
